# Release signing: a template. Copy to `android/keystore.properties` and fill in.
#
#   cp android/keystore.properties.example android/keystore.properties
#
# The real file is gitignored and must stay that way: it holds the passwords to your
# upload key. Keep the keystore itself OUTSIDE this repository (and a copy somewhere
# safe), so that no `git add -A` can ever sweep it in. GitHub releases sign with the
# same four values from repository secrets (.github/workflows/android.yml).
#
# Every app of the suite is signed with the SAME keystore and alias (they share their
# connection on the phone through a permission only same-signed apps hold): each app's
# android/keystore.properties holds the same four values.
#
# Without this file debug builds work (`./gradlew assembleDebug`, `npm run android`), but a
# release build refuses to run: it would be signed with React Native's public debug key.
# `./gradlew assembleRelease -PallowDebugSigning` builds one anyway, only to try it yourself;
# `bundleRelease` always needs the real key. The build also fails loudly if this file is
# present but incomplete.
#
# All four keys are required.

# Absolute path, or a path relative to the `android/` directory.
storeFile=/path/outside/the/repository/unlease-upload.jks

# The keystore password.
storePassword=

# The alias the key was generated under.
keyAlias=upload

# The key password. keytool was run with -keypass equal to -storepass, so for a keystore
# generated by the documented command this is the same value as storePassword.
keyPassword=
